# Hanzi Atlas — project rules

A 3D interactive atlas of Chinese characters (汉字星图). Characters are
stars; shared components draw constellation lines. Read `BRIEF.md` for the
full concept before anything else. Follow `PLAN.md` stage by stage — do only
the stage the user names, then stop and wait.

## Golden rules
- **The user must be able to explain every line at a university defense.**
  Favor clear, readable code over clever code. Add short comments that say
  *why*, not *what*. No unexplained magic numbers.
- Do only the current stage from `PLAN.md`. Do not jump ahead. End each
  stage by printing a short "what changed / how to run / how to verify" note.
- Never invent linguistic data. All character data comes from the real
  sources in the data pipeline (Make Me a Hanzi, CC-CEDICT, Unihan, HSK).
  If data is missing, show a graceful empty state — never fake a definition
  or a stroke order.
- Keep it dependency-light. Three.js r170 + Vite only unless `PLAN.md` asks
  for more. No React, no UI framework, no CSS framework.

## Tech
- Web client: **vanilla Three.js r170**, ES modules, Vite dev server.
- Data is prepared offline by Node scripts in `/scripts` into a compact
  `/public/data/atlas.json`. The client only ever loads the finished JSON —
  no parsing of raw sources in the browser.
- Thousands of glyphs must stay at ~60 FPS: use `InstancedMesh` (or points +
  a glyph atlas texture), not one mesh per character. Build the graph once;
  animate by interpolating positions, never by rebuilding the scene.
- `OrbitControls` for camera. Respect `prefers-reduced-motion`.

## Visual system (do not drift from this)
Deep night-indigo void, aged-paper starlight, cinnabar + celadon accents —
ink-and-cinnabar on old silk, **never neon / cyberpunk**.

```css
:root {
  --ink-night:   #0E1526; /* base background */
  --ink-deep:    #0A101D; /* far plane, vignette */
  --starlight:   #E8E0CE; /* glyphs, primary text */
  --cinnabar:    #C8432E; /* selected char + its constellation lines */
  --celadon:     #6E9887; /* shared-radical highlight */
  --gold-leaf:   #C9A24B; /* key radicals, rare accent */
  --muted:       #8A93A6; /* secondary UI text */
}
```

- Type: **Noto Serif SC** for glyphs + headings (Song style is the
  typographic hero); **Noto Serif** for Latin/pinyin, pinyin in italic.
  Load from Google Fonts. Do NOT use a monospace font for data labels — it's
  a generated-page tell.
- Avoid these tells: ALL-CAPS tracked eyebrow labels; one word in a heading
  accented in a different color; `A · B · C` middle-dot meta strings; a `→`
  glued onto link/button text; identical rounded cards with the same soft
  grey shadow. Structure (borders, dividers, numbers) must encode real
  information, not decorate.
- Motion: exactly one orchestrated moment on load (see BRIEF). Otherwise,
  motion only answers a user action (select, open, switch layout). No
  fade-up on every element.
- Bilingual by default: 中文 label first, English/pinyin as the quiet
  secondary. UI copy is plain and active ("Switch layout", not "Toggle").

## Quality floor (every stage)
Responsive to mobile, visible keyboard focus, reduced-motion respected,
accessible contrast. Don't announce it — just meet it.
